# Project Structure & Architecture

This is a modern real-time chat application built for a hackathon. The project follows a specific architecture and structure that must be maintained.

## Architecture Overview
- **Frontend**: Next.js 15 with App Router (React 19), shadcn/ui components, Tailwind CSS 4
- **Backend**: Firebase (Auth, Firestore, Realtime Database, Storage, Cloud Functions, FCM)
- **Package Manager**: Use `pnpm` and `pnpx` instead of npm/npx
- **Hosting**: Next.js dev server locally, production hosting TBD

## Project Structure
- `app/`: Next.js App Router routes, layouts, pages, server actions
- `lib/`: Shared utilities (Firebase client/server initializers, helpers)
- `public/`: Static assets
- `docs/`: Product and technical documentation (see [PRD.md](mdc:docs/PRD.md) and [general.md](mdc:docs/general.md))
- `.vscode/`: Editor configuration

## Key Files
- Main configuration: [package.json](mdc:package.json), [tsconfig.json](mdc:tsconfig.json), [components.json](mdc:components.json)
- Entry point: [app/layout.tsx](mdc:app/layout.tsx), [app/page.tsx](mdc:app/page.tsx)
- Utilities: [lib/utils.ts](mdc:lib/utils.ts)

## Naming Conventions
- Use kebab-case for files/folders in docs (`docs/features/*`)
- TypeScript throughout the project
- Keep components small and colocate feature-specific UI under app/ routes or feature folders
- Prefer `type` over `interface` for TypeScript definitions

## File Organization Rules
- Keep files under 250 lines of code
- Separate mock data from source code
- Eliminate redundant code
- Colocate related functionality
- Use absolute paths when possible (@/* imports configured)